Changelog: 09-10-2026 11:51:07

install.sh: BC250_LOG_DIR overrides /var/log for testing, like BC250_OPT_DIR for /opt. Without it nothing
changes: --uninstall --purge still removes /var/log/bc250-bazzite-test. The installer tests used to reach the real
folder there, so on a board with results the purge step tried to delete them.

Changelog: 08-10-2026 22:26:29

Moved into the BC250 Bazzite Suite: version 0.1.0, released on the suite's own bazzite-test-v* tags.

The app now uses the suite's shared bc250_core for its palette, Inter font, rounded tooltips, logo, settings
store, help view and start-up. The window looks the same: an offscreen render matches the previous version
pixel for pixel, apart from the version number. Two differences:
- Chart tooltips now open relative to the graph window, like every other tooltip, which is what Wayland needs.
- The app's own copy of the Inter font is gone; core ships the same files.

install.sh copies the bc250_core the release was built with into the root-owned /opt folder and uses the suite's
shared venv in ~/.local/share/bc250-bazzite-suite/venv instead of its own. A venv from an earlier version is
removed. The shared venv is removed with the last suite app that uses it. Settings stay in
~/.config/bc250-bazzite-test/bc250-bazzite-test.ini.

Removed development/tools/release.sh: releases are made by the suite's release workflow. Added offscreen smoke
tests (tests/).

Changelog: 07-10-2026 04:45:00

Game details page: more is measured while a game runs, and the page now judges the session instead of
only showing numbers.

The session CSV gains GTT use, the GPU memory clock and voltage, the CPU temperature and average clock,
the fan speed and the free system memory, so heat, memory pressure and clock droop are visible per game
(the graph window has panels for all of them; older sessions simply show fewer panels).

New "Checks for the last session" panel under the stat chips, with one pass/warn/fail line each for:
smoothness (the real 1% and 0.1% lows and the stutter share from MangoHud's per-frame log, as test 48
computes them), whether the session was GPU- or CPU-bound, VRAM / GTT / RAM headroom against the actual
carve-out, power cap, GPU and CPU temperature and clock droop over the session, and the amdgpu errors
the kernel logged while that game ran. A new "stutter" chip was added, and the "low 1% FPS" chip now
shows the real per-frame low (highlighted) instead of the worst 1% of the sampled averages.

When a game closes the wrapper writes a small .json sidecar next to the session CSV with the memory
budget, the GPU power cap, the frametime statistics and those kernel errors; a failure to write it can
never affect the game or the recording.

Changelog: 07-10-2026 02:40:00

Game details page: the "Style MangoHud" button is renamed to "Set MangoHud overlay style".

Changelog: 07-10-2026 01:45:00

Desktop app install: the release tarball now has an install.sh. Extract the tarball, run ./install.sh as
your own user and start "BC-250 Bazzite Test" from the app menu or the Desktop icon (or the
bc250-bazzite-test command). It copies the app root-owned to /opt/bc250-bazzite-test-v<version>, points
/opt/bc250-bazzite-test at it, removes older versions, sets up the PyQt6 venv and adds the launcher, app
menu entry and Desktop icon (--no-desktop-shortcut skips the icon). Updating is running the new
release's install.sh; --uninstall [--purge] removes it. README install steps and the release workflow
(syntax check of install.sh) updated.

Changelog: 07-10-2026 01:05:00

System score: new Swap item (5 points, Configuration group, read from /proc/swaps and the zswap module
parameter like test 14). zswap in front of a disk swapfile is the default and scores full points, as it is
much more stable for gaming than zram; zram + disk swap or disk swap without zswap 3, zram only or
zram + zswap 2, no usable swap 0. Health score goes from 10 to 5 points to keep the total at 100.
Test 14/37 hints, README, help page and the Swap file recommendation (now: swapfile, zswap enabled,
zram disabled) updated accordingly.
The ⚠ sign behind an item in the System score pop-up is now yellow (#FFD600) instead of white.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 23:42:00

Base System and Extended System boxes swap their colors: #F8FAFC background with #222222 text, in
the stats row and on the Print results card. README and help page now call them light.
The Print results card goes back to its dark background (#14161C, footer text #9AA0A6).

Changelog: 06-10-2026 23:01:00

System score: new Configuration group next to the Hardware group. A board now only scores 100 when it is
also set up as recommended: ACPI fix applied 10 points (cpufreq + cpuidle exist, test 26), VRAM split
6 GB 10 (any other split 5, test 37), CPU mitigations off 5 (kernel command line, test 24), IOMMU
disabled 5 (no IOMMU groups, test 23), BIOS 5 and health score 10. Hardware weights changed to make
room: GPU CUs 20, CPU 15, NVMe read 10, NVMe capacity 5, network link 5. The pop-up shows the two
groups with their subtotals.

Uneven CU unlocks: the CUs are spread over 4 shader arrays and the smallest one sets the pace, so an
uneven distribution is scored as the evenly distributed count below it (4 x the smallest array; without
per-array data the total is rounded down to 24/32/40). The pop-up marks the item and warns that the CUs
are not evenly assigned. Test 21 now logs a WARNING plus HINT when the live WGP masks differ per shader
array, and the history stores the per-array counts (cu.sa0..cu.sa3) from that line. README table and
help page updated.

Print results card follows the new header: the Base System and Extended System scores
are two large tiles under the title, with the nine other boxes as the 3x3 grid below (about 404x297
px). Settings note and README updated.

New colors for the system score boxes: both Base System and Extended System are dark #222222 with
#F8FAFC text, in the stats row and on the Print results card.
The Print results card itself gets a gray background (#6B7280, footer text #E5E7EB) so the dark
system tiles stand out.

Show Logs now colors a stored log like the live terminal (test headers bold, SUCCESS green, INFO blue,
WARNING orange, ERROR red, HINT purple, NOTE gray); CSVs and configs stay plain. The Warnings, Failures
and Info / hints pop-ups get a "Show in log" link per test, next to the log path: it opens that run log
scrolled to the test's [TEST NN] header. Clicking the test name still opens its page.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 22:27:00

New "Base System" (teal) and "Extended System" (pink) score boxes at the end of the stats row, in the
same design as the other boxes (clickable, accent outline on hover). All boxes are narrower now
(minimum width follows their title) so the two extra ones fit at the default window size. A stock BC-250 on
BIOS 5.00 scores exactly 100: GPU CUs 25 points (default 24), CPU 20 (6C/12T; cores 75%, threads 25%),
NVMe sequential read 15 (850 MB/s, test 43), BIOS 10 (5.00; 3.00 = 6, modded counts as current),
NVMe capacity 10 (512 GB class; 480-512 GB drives score exactly 10, above that only whole
size classes count: 1 TB = 12.5, 2 TB = 15, 8 TB = 20), wired network link 10 (1 Gbit/s) and health score 10. Below a
default costs points proportionally; above it only adds to the Extended score (linear for CUs and
cores, +25% of the weight per doubling for drive speed, capacity and link), so the Base score is
capped at 100 and is rounded down - an older BIOS can never reach 100. Clicking a box opens a
pop-up with a score ring and a per-item breakdown; items not measured yet score 0 and are listed
with the test to run. Settings > System overview gets a "BIOS version" box (DMI, with vendor and date
in the tooltip), completing a 3x3 grid. Both scores show a gray "—" until something is measured.

The network default stays at 1 Gbit/s, the onboard port's maximum, so a stock board's Extended score
equals its Base score. The fastest wired link counts, so a USB 2.5/5 GbE adapter raises the Extended
score (2.5 GbE = 13.3 points). README gets a "System score" section with the full table (items,
points, defaults and how going above a default is scored); the help page describes both buttons.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 13:26:00

"Print results" moved out of the General page into its own settings section, listed under Help
in the sidebar: a short explanation of the shareable card plus the button.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 13:10:00

New "Print results" in Settings > General (Share section): saves a compact shareable
"BC-250 Bazzite Test Results" card as a PNG - the logo and title on top, the nine header boxes
(tests/finished/passed, warnings/failures/info, health/CPU/GPU score) as a readable 3x3 grid in
their live colors, and the app version plus date in the footer. A save dialog defaults to
~/Downloads/results.png; the card is about 404x231 px, made for comparing results with other
BC-250 users.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 12:45:00

Version shown in the window no longer lags the GitHub tags. Releases are tagged by CI
(release.yml auto-bumps the patch number on every push to main), but __version__ in
bc250_gui/__init__.py had stayed at 0.12.0, so a checkout run from source showed 0.12.0 while
GitHub was at v0.12.8. __version__ is now 0.12.8 (matching the latest tag; the next push still
releases v0.12.9 as CI counts from the newest of tag/__version__). New helper
development/tools/release.sh: checks __version__ against the tags, predicts the next release
version with the same rule as CI, flags drift in both directions (behind = stale window title,
ahead at patch level = CI skips a number), compile-checks the GUI, and --sync sets __version__
to the latest tag.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 12:40:00

The in-game overlay can now be placed anywhere: click "Test overlay" on the Game details page and
drag the demo overlay with the mouse (open/closed-hand cursor, "drag to move" hint, demo now stays
45 s). The position is saved to ~/.config/bc250-bazzite-test/overlay-position.json and the real
in-game overlay appears there. In-game the overlay stays click-through on purpose - it can never
steal a mouse click from the game - which is why positioning happens in the test overlay. A saved
position from another resolution is clamped back onto the screen. Tooltips and README updated.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 12:30:00

Game details page revamp: the page now has a body. The one-time Steam setup (launch lines,
Enable FPS logging, Style MangoHud) is folded into one compact bordered card at the top. The
selected game is the main content: the screenshot is a 420x236 hero banner with the game title
in large type, playtime and "Last played" next to it, together with the per-game controls
(record, overlay, sample interval). Below the banner a row of stat chips shows hard numbers
from the newest session - number of sessions, average FPS (purple), low 1% FPS (worst 1% of
samples), average GPU load, GPU temperature and GPU power - each with a tooltip explaining the
number; chips show an em dash when the data is not in the log (FPS needs the MangoHud launch
line). The sessions list fills the rest of the pane.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 12:20:00

Settings: the Help content moved from the General page to its own "Help" section, listed
under About in the settings sidebar. The General page keeps only the language, notification
and report-copy options.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 12:15:00

Performance page: the "Save as baseline" checkbox is now grayed out when a baseline already
exists (bench-baseline.json), so the stock-board baseline cannot be overwritten by accident.
The tooltip explains that deleting the baseline file re-enables it. The checkbox state is
refreshed after every benchmark run.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 12:05:00

Test 47 false warning fix: vulkaninfo lists llvmpipe as an extra device on every healthy system,
so the "no hardware Vulkan device" check fired even when test 20 was green. The test now carves
out the first hardware GPU's own block and judges only that: the API version and the feature
matrix can no longer pick up llvmpipe's extensions (llvmpipe exposes e.g. mesh shaders, which
would have faked a "yes"). The judged device name is printed, and the apiVersion parsing handles
the "= 4210945 (1.3.289)" format.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 12:00:00

Test 50 diagnosis fix: "vainfo returned no profiles" was raised without saying why. The test now
keeps vainfo's stderr (where libva reports the failure reason), checks all kernel VCN lines
instead of only the "decode and encode initialized" one, and separates the two causes: when the
kernel initialized no VCN block at all, the missing profiles are reported as a hardware fact
(INFO, video decodes on the CPU, nothing to fix); only when VCN did initialize but vainfo still
exposes nothing is it a warning, with libva's own error lines and a hint to install
mesa-va-drivers-freeworld when radeonsi_drv_video.so is missing (Fedora strips H.264/HEVC from
its Mesa VA-API driver).

