Changelog: 06-10-2026 11:45:00

Four new tests and two extensions aimed at hard evidence of the system's gaming limits and
futureproofness:

- Test 47 Vulkan Futureproof Matrix: vulkaninfo-based feature matrix (API level vs what DXVK 2.x /
  vkd3d-proton require, ray tracing, mesh shaders, VRS, graphics pipeline library) with factual
  verdicts: missing hardware RT means RT-required titles (Indiana Jones: The Great Circle, Doom:
  The Dark Ages) cannot run at all; missing mesh shaders flags future-title risk; the VRAM
  carveout is checked against today's 8 GiB AAA recommendation.
- Test 48 Real-Game Frametime Analysis: reads the most recent MangoHud logs (output_folder from
  the user's MangoHud.conf, plus common fallbacks, all read as the desktop user): average FPS,
  1% and 0.1% lows (average of the worst frames), session length and the share of frames over 2x
  the median. Warns when the 1% low falls under half the average (real stutter), with BC-250
  specific hints. Explains how to enable logging when no logs exist.
- Test 49 CPU Memory Bandwidth: stress-ng STREAM-like bandwidth on 1 thread and all threads, with
  a scaling verdict; quantifies the GDDR6 latency the CPU pays, which explains why
  single-thread-bound games lose more on this board than compute benchmarks suggest.
- Test 50 Video Decode and Encode Matrix: vainfo codec matrix (H.264, HEVC, VP9, AV1 x
  decode/encode) plus the kernel's VCN init line; notes CPU fallback for AV1 streams and whether
  OBS can record through VA-API.
- Test 41 now gives a sustained-performance verdict on runs of 120 s and longer: average clock in
  the first loaded minute vs the last minute; under 97% is thermal sag, under 90% a throttling
  warning with airflow hints.
- Test 42 now prints a comparison ladder after the baseline score: the measured FP32 GFLOPS as a
  percentage of published theoretical peaks of known systems (Steam Deck, Xbox Series S, stock
  BC-250 ceiling, announced Steam Machine, PS5, RTX 4060/4090), with explicit caveats that FP32
  positioning is not an FPS prediction and that RDNA3 dual-issue peaks overstate the reference.

catalog.py, both READMEs' test tables and the test 41/42 descriptions updated accordingly.

Changelog: 06-10-2026 10:42:00

IOMMU without a modded BIOS: new README section "Disabling the IOMMU without a modded BIOS"
documenting the amd_iommu=off kernel arg (rpm-ostree kargs --append=amd_iommu=off) as the
software-only alternative to the modded 3.00 BIOS's Advanced Chipset menu, including
verification, nuances (functionally equivalent to the BIOS toggle, not a hardware disable,
iommu=pt is not sufficient, VFIO passthrough lost) and a note that test 23 validates either
route since it counts IOMMU groups. Added a "Chipset menu → IOMMU disable" row to the
modded-BIOS equivalents table. Test 23 wording in test-bazzite.sh (log lines and error hint),
bc250_gui/catalog.py and both READMEs' test tables now mention the kernel-arg alternative
instead of "must be disabled in the BIOS".

Changelog: 06-10-2026 09:42:00

