Changelog: 29-09-2026 20:55:00

TEST 41 — first real GPU stress run: memtest_vulkan (2.1 GB) kept the GPU 89% busy at 1850 MHz
for 120 s, ~120 W, GPU 57 C / CPU 69 C, no VRAM errors, no faults.
- Verdict fix: a clock held at the governor's ceiling for the whole run was flagged as "the
  governor is not reacting". It is now a SUCCESS when the clock stays within 100 MHz of the
  governor maximum; the warning remains for a clock stuck low.

TEST 40 — governor telemetry settings:
- Reports gpu.set-method (smu/kernel), gpu-usage.method and fix-metrics (defaults as upstream:
  smu, busy-flag, true).
- fix-metrics on: checks the patched gpu_metrics is mounted (WARNING + restart hint if not).
  fix-metrics off: INFO plus NOTE hints on how to enable it (overlays show 0%/655% otherwise).
- set-method "kernel": NOTE that a stock driver caps it at 2000 MHz and smu is the usual choice.
- README: set-method explained in the fix-metrics section.

Swap check:
- With zram plus disk swap, the check now verifies that zram has the higher priority (so the
  disk swap really is the emergency backstop) and hints at pri=10 in /etc/fstab if not.

Changelog: 29-09-2026 20:15:00

Logs:
- All output now goes to /var/log/bc250-bazzite-test/ and is copied to
  ~/Desktop/bc250-bazzite-test/ (the GPU load tool log is copied too).

TEST 41 — memtest_vulkan filled the RAM and the OOM killer took the desktop:
- On this APU memtest_vulkan sizes its test from free system RAM (minus 400 MB): it allocated
  9.6 GB of GTT, the OOM killer killed kwin_wayland, the portal and the script, and memtest kept
  running in its own session until a hard reset (its VRAM test itself passed).
- memtest_vulkan now gets "0 <max bytes>" (autoselect device, non-interactive): 3/4 of the VRAM
  carve-out, at most half of MemAvailable.
- The GPU load tool sets its oom_score_adj to 1000 (first OOM victim instead of the desktop) and
  runs under timeout, so it stops even if the script is killed.
- The script sets its own oom_score_adj to -500 during the run (restored afterwards).
- Memory guard: the run stops when MemAvailable drops below 5% of RAM (min 512 MiB); the
  "cut short" warning names the guard that fired.

Changelog: 29-09-2026 19:50:00

TEST 41 — keep the evidence of a lockup:
- The GPU load tool's output moved from /tmp (lost on reboot) to /var/log/bc250-gpuload-<ts>.log.
- The CSV, the results log and the GPU tool log are synced to disk after every sample, so the
  last seconds before a hard lockup survive the reboot.

Changelog: 29-09-2026 19:30:00

TEST 41 — the GPU load tool itself was the bottleneck:
- Correction: CPU starvation was not the main cause. With SCHED_IDLE + autogroup nice 19 the
  result was the same, and the vkmark log showed ~2000 FPS in every scene at 1080p. The busy
  peaks (~60%) every 40 s were the effect2d blur scene coming round in vkmark's 4-scene cycle,
  including the one that coincided with stress-ng ending. The low CPU priority is kept anyway.
- GPU load preference is now memtest_vulkan -> vkpeak -> vkmark -> glmark2 -> vkcube (same as
  bc250-cu-bisect.sh). memtest_vulkan and vkpeak are headless compute loads that keep the GPU busy;
  memtest_vulkan's "Error found" lines are reported as an ERROR (VRAM errors under load).
- The desktop user's ~/.local/bin is searched too (sudo resets PATH).
  The GPU load tool is started by its full path, because "sudo -u <user> env" resets PATH
  again ("env: memtest_vulkan: No such file or directory").
- vkmark fallback: 2560x1440 window, only effect2d blur and desktop:windows=16:window-size=0.8.
- The fdinfo busy measurement now counts the compute engine as well (the busier of gfx and
  compute is reported), since compute tools may use the compute ring.
- The "never meaningfully loaded" warning recommends memtest_vulkan when a frame-based tool ran.

Changelog: 29-09-2026 19:05:00

TEST 41 — nice 19 was not enough (board run: busy 67% at t=0, then 10-25% at 1000 MHz once
the load average reached 12):
- sched_autogroup (on by default on Fedora/Bazzite) gives every setsid session an equal CPU
  share regardless of nice, so the niced stress-ng still took ~half of the CPU that vkmark ran on.
  Each CPU worker now sets its own autogroup to nice 19 (from inside the new session, so the
  script's group is untouched) and runs as SCHED_IDLE via chrt -i 0 (nice 19 fallback). The
  log line shows which priority settings were applied.

Changelog: 29-09-2026 19:15:00

TEST 41 — GPU stress load was too light (board run: ~10% busy, sclk stuck at 1000 MHz):
- The CPU load (stress-ng/stress/busy-loops on every thread) now runs at nice 19. At normal
  priority it starved vkmark's render thread: busy jumped from ~6% to ~37% the moment
  stress-ng timed out. The CPU load timeout now covers the whole sampling window (it used to
  end ~5 s early); cleanup stops it.
- vkmark runs in a 1920x1080 window with only the heavy scenes (effect2d blur, shading phong,
  texture anisotropy=16, desktop); glmark2 runs at 1920x1080. The defaults (800x600) render
  frames in 0.05-0.5 ms and are bound by the CPU and presentation, not the GPU.

Changelog: 29-09-2026 18:30:00

TEST 41 — GPU load measurement:
- Stock kernels have no GPU-load sensor for Cyan Skillfish: gpu_busy_percent fails with
  EOPNOTSUPP and was silently read as 0, so every stress run reported "0% busy" even with
  vkmark rendering (same root cause as MangoHud / Steam overlay showing 0% when the
  governor's fix-metrics is off). The busy % now comes from gpu_busy_percent only when it
  is readable, otherwise from per-client drm-engine-gfx time in /proc/*/fdinfo (clients
  present in both samples only). The source is logged.
- "GPU was never meaningfully loaded" is now a WARNING (the test setup did not load the
  GPU; that is not a fault of the system). Its hint no longer suggests the non-existent
  --gpu-load=none option and points at the GPU load tool's log instead.

TEST 30 — SSH not running is now INFO (the Bazzite default), with the enable command as a NOTE.

README:
- New section: "Fix: GPU load shows 0% in the Steam overlay / MangoHud" (governor fix-metrics).

