  The duplicate-tables warning as a note block

Changelog: 28-09-2026 19:47:00

TEST 36 false ERROR fixed (benign amdgpu WARN reported as kernel fault):
- Line-based filtering stripped the RIP/dal_irq lines of the benign DC-layer
  WARN_ON at probe but left its orphaned "Call Trace:" lines, raising an
  ERROR (and an overall FAILURE verdict) on every run, with no context to
  judge it by.
- The panic scan now groups each hit into a block with 8 lines of leading
  and 6 lines of trailing context, and drops whole blocks carrying a
  known-benign signature. Adjacent blocks are split on gaps, so a benign
  WARN next to a real oops can no longer suppress the real one.
- Surviving traces are printed with their context, making them diagnosable
  straight from the log.

Journal noise filters (TESTs 15/16), all verified against a real run:
- utmp/clock/tty/kmem/input/sgx group-resolution failures (bootc early-boot
  ordering, cosmetic).
- "Failed to parse ACL" from systemd-tmpfiles on bootc images.
- powerdevil DDC/CI brightness probes returning i2c EREMOTEIO on monitors
  without DDC support.
- nm_dispatcher activation failure (not shipped activatable on Bazzite).
- "x86 CPU feature dependency check failure ... pcid" (BC-250 BIOS quirk).
- "ACPI BIOS Warning" (FADT Pm2ControlBlock).

TEST 21:
- 25-39 CUs is now a SUCCESS labeled as an intentional partial unlock,
  with a note on why partial unlocks are normal, instead of
  an ambiguous "partial unlock, or unhealthy CUs have been masked".

TEST 00:
— BIOS identification (via /sys/class/dmi/id, no dmidecode needed):
  Reports vendor, version, date, and release
  lassifies the version string: stock ASRock (1.00–5.00, with optional L/P prefix),
  modded (MeiMei/mod/unlock in the string) with a hint not to combine it with the
  initrd ACPI override, missing (WARNING), or unrecognized

TEST 26
— ACPI table origin: checks the kernel log for ACPI:
  Table Upgrade / SSDT (bootloader) entries, so you can tell "fix not applied"
  apart from "fix applied but failed" — and see whether tables came from initrd
  (bc250-acpi-fix) or the BIOS itself.

README:
- TEST 21 description updated: active CUs come from the CU manager's state /
  the saved boot table (24 stock, up to 40 unlocked), with the probe-time
  kernel/RADV caveat. All other test descriptions verified against the
  script (42 tests, numbers and titles match).
- TEST 00 row updated to match. Syntax and classification patterns verified.

Changelog: 28-09-2026 19:29:00

Changes to TEST 21
1. New system-wide check: reads the CU manager's config (mask popcount × 2 = 28) and verifies its service is enabled — matches your setup exactly.
2. Fixed cache path/parser: correct Data/ location, parses the authoritative CUs active & routed : X / 40 line plus Boot sync : current table saved.
3. Corrected messaging: kernel/RADV labeled probe-time; RADV mismatch downgraded from WARNING to an explanatory INFO (harmless, dispatch uses all routed CUs).

Changelog: 28-09-2026 19:01:00

Bugfix for live CU changes:
The script reads active_cu_number from the kernel log — which amdgpu prints once at driver probe during boot, so a live change was invisible to it.

TEST 21 now, in priority order:

- The CU manager's live state file → live cu_active_cus, plus whether cu_boot_saved is set (warns if the change is live-only and will be lost on cold boot).
- Its per-user dashboard cache as a fallback.
- Kernel log — now explicitly labeled as the boot-time value, reported alongside rather than overriding the live one.
- If only 24 is found from the kernel, it now prints a NOTE explaining the live/boot distinction instead of silently claiming stock.

The RADV mismatch warning now explains itself too: Mesa caches the CU count when it opens the device, so anything already running keeps the old value until restarted.

Changelog: 28-09-2026 16:18:00

Add gaming, crash-forensics and stress tests; fix false positives

Extends the BC-250/Bazzite test script from 34 to 42 tests, focused on
diagnosing mid-game hard lockups.

New tests:
- 34 Previous boot shutdown integrity, pstore panic records
- 35 GPU hang/reset history (ring timeouts, VM faults, recovery params)
- 36 Kernel panics, lockups, coredumps, NMI watchdog state
- 37 Unified memory: VRAM/GTT use, OOM history, framebuffer pin failures
- 38 Steam, gamescope, gamemode, MangoHud, library free space
- 39 PipeWire/WirePlumber state, default sink, ALSA cards
- 40 GPU power cap, governor frequency ceiling, throttle messages
- 41 Opt-in CPU+GPU stress run with telemetry (--stress[=SECONDS])

Fixed false positives found in real runs:
- Clean shutdowns reported as hard lockups. journald stops before the
  final power-off line is written, so detect that shutdown began
  (Stopped target / Unmounting) instead of that it completed.
- Benign amdgpu dal_irq_service WARN_ON traces and the "NMI watchdog:
  Enabled" banner no longer match the panic scan.
- Filter "Unsupported screen format RA24" and irq_service.c warnings.

Stress mode:
- Session environment is read from the running compositor via
  /proc/<pid>/environ rather than guessing WAYLAND_DISPLAY, which made
  the GPU load tool exit instantly and silently.
- Verify the load process survives startup; report its stderr if not.
- Sample gpu_busy_percent, vram_used and gtt_used; a run averaging
  under 50% busy is reported as invalid instead of passing.
- Telemetry CSV is copied to the desktop alongside the report.

Other:
- TEST 00 flags kernels 6.15.0-6.15.6 and 6.17.8-6.17.10, known to
  cause GPU crashes that take the whole APU down.
- TEST 14 now reports the full swap topology: zram sizing relative to
  the unified pool, zswap parameters, disk swap, Btrfs NOCOW checks,
  zram+zswap overlap and swappiness suitability.
- Boot scans widened from 5 to 10 retained boots.
- README documents all 42 tests, both output files and VRAM guidance.
